As you may recall, limit processes are central to the development of calculus. 
The mathematical and philosophoical debate in the 18th and 19th centuries surronding the meaning and soundness of techniques of taking limits was intense, questioning the very foundations of calculus.
By the middle of the 19th century, the infamous "∈ - δ" definition of limits had been devised, chiefly by Karl Weierstrass and Augustin Cauchy, much to the chagrin of many 20th (and 21th) century students of calculus. 
In the ensuing discussion, we study both the intuitive and rigorous meaning of the limit of a function f: X ⊆ R'' -> R''' and how limits lead to the notion of a continuous function, our main object of study he remander of this repo
